 ## Overview
-Deformation Field Modeling addresses the problem of reconstructing and rendering dynamic scenes from static image sequences by mapping time-varying coordinates to a static canonical space. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by explicitly parameterizing the geometric transformation $\Psi_t$ that warps dynamic observations into a reference configuration, allowing standard static radiance field or Gaussian splatting renderers to process temporal data without modifying the core rendering pipeline.
+Deformation Field Modeling addresses the problem of reconstructing and rendering dynamic scenes from static image or video sequences by explicitly modeling the temporal evolution of geometry and appearance. It solves the limitation of static radiance fields by mapping dynamic, time-varying coordinates to a static canonical space where the radiance field is defined. What sets this approach apart from siblings like [Dynamic Object Embedding in Static Scenes](8796-dynamic-object-embedding-in-static-scenes.md) (which isolates motion to specific objects) or [Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting](9168-dynamic-3d-gaussian-splatting-katsumata-et-al.md) (which uses explicit parametric functions) is its reliance on a learned implicit function (typically an MLP) to predict continuous displacement vectors or feature transformations, allowing for complex, non-rigid, and global deformations without assuming a specific motion prior [S1, S2].
 
