 ## Background
-Deformation field modeling originated with the introduction of Nerfies by Park et al. in November 2020, which applied deformation fields to handle non-rigid transformations in dynamic scenes [S2]. Concurrently, D-NeRF introduced similar canonical space mappings for dynamic radiance fields [S1]. The approach has since been adapted to 3D Gaussian Splatting, with methods like Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting and SWAGS extending the concept to explicit geometric primitives [S1]. Applications include medical imaging, such as markless motion reconstruction for infants [S1], and monocular endoscopic reconstruction [S1].
+The concept of deformation fields for dynamic scenes originated with the introduction of Nerfies by Park et al. in November 2020 [S2]. Nerfies introduced an additional MLP to map input observation frame coordinates to deformed canonical coordinates, enabling the reconstruction of non-rigid transformations that baseline NeRFs failed to handle [S2]. This was followed by concurrent work like NerFace (December 2020) and subsequent extensions like HyperNeRF (June 2021) [S2]. In the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, methods like SWAGS have adapted these principles by dividing sequences into motion-based windows and using tunable MLPs for localized deformation [S1]. These techniques are applied in various fields, including medical motion analysis and endoscopic reconstruction [S1].
 
